{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And now, forgive my sin, please, just this once, and entreat YHWH your Elohim.</b> Ramban, of blessed memory, explained: it would have been fitting for him to say, 'entreat YHWH that He forgive my sin,' for Moses does not bear iniquity; but he mentioned this to Moses because he is as Elohim to Pharaoh. And he said והעתירו in the plural, as a mark of honor for Aaron, for Pharaoh knew that Moses alone does the entreating, since he had already said to him several times, והעתרתי אל ה׳ — and I will entreat YHWH — and, למתי אעתיר לך — when shall I entreat for you — and it is written, ויעתר אל ה׳ — and he entreated YHWH." "<b>Only this death.</b> This teaches you that people were dying in the plague of locusts just as they were dying in the plague of hail, and perhaps the locusts would blind their eyes, like the matter of the hornet in the time of Joshua. Or one may say, את המות הזה — 'this death' — because all the produce of the land was blasted by the hail, and afterwards the locusts came to consume what the hail had left, and after these two plagues no sustenance remained for the people of the land, and so this was death for them. And he used the word רק — 'only' — because many times it happened to Pharaoh that he would appease Moses' face to entreat concerning the plague, and when the plague ceased he would go back and harden his heart; therefore he now says: I do not ask you to entreat for me for all time, but that you entreat this one time only, to remove from me this death — and this is the meaning of רק, to limit the other times."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:57:40.950814785Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}